  "query_preview": "..Utah and southwest Wyoming remain between two\ntroughs this morning on satellite imagery, resulting in largely \nquiescent conditions under a dry, stable air mass. Isolated areas\nof fog developed early this morning given calm and clear\nconditions, but is unlikely to return tonight given increasing\nmid-level clouds ahead of the trough to our west that will limit\nsurface cooling.\n\nAs that trough continues to close off over the Great Basin,\nprecipitation chances will increase first over the West Desert by\nearly Wednesday morning, shifting over northern/central Utah by\nlate morning. Low-level flow will become more northeasterly\nthrough the day, moreso favoring measurable precipitation over the\nWasatch Back and areas east rather than the Wasatch Front. Still,\nprecipitation amounts will be minimal, with the 75th percentile \n(25% chance of exceedance) showing around 0.25\" of rainfall near \nPark City/Heber City. High temperatures tomorrow will be around\n5-10 degrees below normal as this system brings cooler air into\nthe region.\n\nFollowing this storm system, this cooler, yet dry air mass will \nstick around through the work week as largely cyclonic flow \ndominates the western US. As skies clear later this week, we will\nneed to monitor valley fog chances, particularly given above- \nnormal soil moisture across the area. Over the weekend, a steady \nwarming trend is expected as high pressure builds, with\ntemperatures approaching 5-10 degrees above normal by Monday. \nEnsemble guidance suggests our next precipitation chances won't \nreturn until at least the middle of next week.",
